    "design_critique": "1. Speed is raised without evidence that the robot can hold the line at that speed. base_speed 1.7 gives base PWM 3480 (85%), which is arithmetically correct. But the design has no estimate of ground speed, lateral acceleration, or tyre slip on the tightest bend in the allowed course. The rationale says the PWM-to-speed mapping is unknown, yet it still predicts a 2.5 to 3.0 s finish with no derivation. Off-track ends the run, and an off-track run loses the 500-point completion bonus and most of the progress terms. The time bonus is only 60*(1 - t/3), so going from 3.0 s to 2.5 s gains about 10 points. The risk side is far larger, so the speed increase is not justified by the scoring rule as written.

2. The control gains were not rescaled for the higher speed. At 1.7 m/s the robot travels about 17 mm per 10 ms step, and the sensors are 0.05 m ahead, so the look-ahead time is about 30 ms. kp = 2.0 was kept unchanged. A higher speed means the same turn command produces a larger path change per step, so the loop gain relative to speed has effectively increased. The design does not check whether the loop is still stable at 1.7.

3. The derivative change is mis-described. kd acts on the rate of change of the signal, so it damps the steering response. It does not make the robot react earlier to a bend. With kd = 0.10 and kp = 2.0, the derivative term is small relative to the proportional term for slow signal changes. For fast changes it is not small, and it amplifies sensor noise (the contract adds
